WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.235 --> 00:00:21.268
Buck's ten-year-old sister, Sheila, stared at the Doggie Express van. She was short and stocky like Buck.

2
00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:28.674
"Your mom's van is so cool, Patrick!" Sheila said. "I love dogs. Don't you?"

3
00:00:28.675 --> 00:00:36.660
Patrick followed Sheila as she walked around the van. "Sure, I like dogs," he said.

4
00:00:36.661 --> 00:00:41.500
"My brother has a mutt named Buff that can catch a Frisbee and do lots of tricks.

5
00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:48.156
But Buff is scared to death of my cat, Slider. Slider is half his size and still rules our house!"

6
00:00:48.157 --> 00:00:51.577
Sheila laughed. "I like her name."

7
00:00:51.578 --> 00:00:57.034
By now Mrs. Deboe was surrounded by other parents asking her about the dog-grooming business.

8
00:00:57.035 --> 00:01:04.637
His mother was small and wiry, like Patrick. All he could see were the red curls bouncing on top of her head.

9
00:01:04.638 --> 00:01:10.463
Suddenly a real howl came from the back of the van. "A-roo!"

10
00:01:10.464 --> 00:01:14.253
"My mother must already have a customer in there," said Patrick.

11
00:01:14.254 --> 00:01:17.461
"A-roo! A-roo!"

12
00:01:17.462 --> 00:01:21.469
"It sounds like an unhappy customer!" said Sheila.

13
00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:26.787
Patrick and Sheila peeked into the back window of the van. A dog was in a crate.

14
00:01:26.788 --> 00:01:31.025
"He looks sad. Can I pet him?" asked Sheila.

15
00:01:31.026 --> 00:01:36.774
"Sure," said Patrick. "Maybe you can cheer him up until my mom gets done talking to people."

16
00:01:36.775 --> 00:01:43.259
He opened the van's back door, and suddenly the door of the crate sprang open too.

17
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.029
Patrick yelped as a cocker spaniel bounded out of the van.

18
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:53.359
"Catch him!" yelled Daniel, running over to help.

19
00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:01.249
But the dog wasn't going anywhere. He danced maniacally around Sheila and Patrick.

20
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.289
Sheila laughed. "He likes us!"

21
00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:09.733
"He sure does." Daniel picked up the pup and let Sheila pet him for a few minutes.

22
00:02:09.734 --> 00:02:15.589
Then he put the dog back in the crate and made sure the door was locked this time.

23
00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:21.692
"Thanks for capturing Otto, Daniel," said Mrs. Deboe as they all got in the van to head home.

24
00:02:21.693 --> 00:02:25.330
"You saved me from losing one of my first clients."

25
00:02:25.331 --> 00:02:29.390
Daniel grinned. "I am a catcher, you know."

26
00:02:29.391 --> 00:02:36.065
Mrs. Deboe laughed. "You can catch baseballs—and pets!"

27
00:02:36.066 --> 00:02:41.038
Everyone but Patrick laughed again.

28
00:02:41.039 --> 00:02:51.365
He stayed silent, staring out the window as his teammates barked at the Doggie Express van driving out of the parking lot.

29
00:02:51.366 --> 00:02:54.846
"What happened to my great day?" he thought.

30
00:02:54.847 --> 00:03:00.287
The first practice had been disappointing, and he had a pile of math homework waiting for him.

31
00:03:00.288 --> 00:03:07.542
Plus, no one in his family seemed to care that a new catcher—a really good new catcher—had joined the Dragons.

32
00:03:07.543 --> 00:03:13.629
"And, according to Daniel," Patrick thought, "I absolutely stink as a catcher!"

33
00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:21.445
During dinner Mrs. Deboe kept talking about her new business. "I got three jobs this afternoon just in the parking lot!

34
00:03:21.446 --> 00:03:25.338
I think Doggie Express is going to be a big success!"

35
00:03:25.339 --> 00:03:29.487
"We'll help when we can—right, boys?" Mr. Deboe said.

36
00:03:29.488 --> 00:03:32.544
"Woof!" Daniel grinned.

37
00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:40.434
"Sure," muttered Patrick. He was glad that his mom had a new business. But her dog van looked ridiculous.

38
00:03:40.435 --> 00:03:44.771
He didn't want to ride in it, especially not to and from baseball games.

39
00:03:44.772 --> 00:03:48.514
His teammates would never stop barking and howling at it.

40
00:03:48.515 --> 00:03:53.504
Half an hour later, Patrick and Daniel were washing dishes.

41
00:03:53.505 --> 00:04:00.089
"You want to go over some of the batters we'll be facing at the scrimmage next week?" asked Daniel when they were almost finished.

42
00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:04.419
"I'd like to sit in on that!" called Mr. Deboe from the living room.

43
00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:10.489
Mrs. Deboe frowned. "Patrick, don't you have math homework?" she asked.

44
00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:19.489
Patrick hesitated. He'd planned on starting his math homework after dinner, but a baseball strategy session sounded like a lot more fun.

45
00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:22.225
"Don't worry, Mom," he said quickly.

46
00:04:22.226 --> 00:04:28.412
"It's the weekend. I've got plenty of time for math. I can work on my homework later and all day tomorrow."

47
00:04:28.413 --> 00:04:34.037
"Okay," she said with a nod. "Just remember our discussion about math.

48
00:04:34.038 --> 00:04:36.787
You can't play baseball if your grades slip."

49
00:04:36.788 --> 00:04:41.951
"No problem," Patrick said. "I'm totally on top of the situation."

50
00:04:41.952 --> 00:04:47.687
Daniel sat at the computer while Patrick and Mr. Deboe pulled up chairs beside him.

51
00:04:47.688 --> 00:04:54.964
Daniel quickly found the roster for the team the Dragons were playing next week, the Swallows.

52
00:04:54.965 --> 00:05:02.011
Patrick scanned through the batters' names. In baseball, the catcher was the only player who could see the whole field.

53
00:05:02.012 --> 00:05:08.535
So it was his job to know where everyone was—his own players as well as any runners on base.

54
00:05:08.536 --> 00:05:15.693
It was a big advantage for a catcher to know about the players on the other team since the catcher was the one who called the pitches.

55
00:05:15.694 --> 00:05:20.856
Daniel pointed at Number 14. "This player crowds the base," he said.

56
00:05:20.857 --> 00:05:26.504
"Give him a pitch on the inside of the plate. He'll hit an outside pitch out of the park."

57
00:05:26.505 --> 00:05:33.424
"I remember him," said Mr. Deboe. "He hit that pop-up you caught in the first game last year."

58
00:05:33.425 --> 00:05:37.326
Patrick wasn't surprised that his father remembered a play from a year ago.

59
00:05:37.327 --> 00:05:42.492
Mr. Deboe practically lived on ballpark hotdogs and popcorn during baseball season.

60
00:05:42.493 --> 00:05:47.576
Patrick hoped that his father would soon be talking about Patrick's middle school plays too.

61
00:05:47.577 --> 00:05:51.978
"This one, Number 42, is a seventh-grader," continued Daniel.

62
00:05:51.979 --> 00:05:56.887
"He's a good hitter," Patrick murmured. "I remember him from Little League."

63
00:05:56.888 --> 00:06:01.543
Mr. Deboe leaned forward. "Think he can hit Dell's pitches?"

64
00:06:01.544 --> 00:06:06.651
"He may find that middle school pitchers are a lot tougher than what he's used to," said Daniel.

65
00:06:06.652 --> 00:06:15.136
It was after nine o'clock when Mrs. Deboe pointed to the clock. "Okay, Patrick, enough baseball. It's time for math."

66
00:06:15.137 --> 00:06:21.452
"Coming," Patrick murmured. He stood up a few minutes later and Daniel gave him a thumbs-up.

67
00:06:21.453 --> 00:06:25.472
"Work on learning the batters every night, and you'll be ready for the game."

68
00:06:25.473 --> 00:06:30.525
Mr. Deboe pointed to his head. "Half the game is in here," he said.

69
00:06:30.526 --> 00:06:36.477
"A catcher's got to be smart." He turned to Daniel. "Want to go out to the garage for a bit?"

70
00:06:36.478 --> 00:06:38.620
Daniel grinned. "Sure."

71
00:06:38.621 --> 00:06:46.216
Patrick wasn't surprised. Almost every night Daniel and Mr. Deboe worked on an old car that Mr. Deboe had bought last year.

72
00:06:46.217 --> 00:06:49.467
They both loved tinkering with engines.

73
00:06:49.468 --> 00:06:54.765
Patrick said good night and headed upstairs.

74
00:06:54.766 --> 00:07:00.114
He brushed his teeth, grabbed his math book, and plopped down on the bed.

75
00:07:00.115 --> 00:07:06.000
Maybe he needed to fill half his brain with baseball, but the other half had to be full of math.

76
00:07:06.001 --> 00:07:12.079
By 10:30 algebra formulas were swimming across the book pages like fish in the sea.

77
00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.238
"Plenty of time for math homework tomorrow," thought Patrick.

78
00:07:16.239 --> 00:07:25.213
A minute later he was sound asleep.
